.TH GREP 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
grep \- search text for lines matching a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grep
.RB [ \-E | \-F ]
.RB [ \-c | \-l | \-q ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.BI \-e " pattern_list"
.RI [ \-e " pattern_list" ]\ .\|.\|.
.RI [ \-f " pattern_file" ]\ .\|.\|.
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B grep
.RB [ \-E | \-F ]
.RB [ \-c | \-l | \-q ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.RI [ \-e " pattern_list" ]\ .\|.\|.
.BI \-f " pattern_file"
.RI [ \-f " pattern_file" ]\ .\|.\|.
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B grep
.RB [ \-E | \-F ]
.RB [ \-c | \-l | \-q ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.RB [ \-n ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-w ]
.RB [ \-x ]
.I pattern_list
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grep
reads each
.I file
operand (or standard input, if none are given, or if a
.I file
operand is exactly
.BR \- ),
and writes to standard output every line that matches at least one of
the collected patterns, or, if
.B \-v
is given, every line that matches none of them.
.PP
Patterns are collected from exactly one of three sources, chosen by
what is given on the command line: one or more
.B \-e
options, one or more
.B \-f
options, or, only when neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
is given at all, a single
.I pattern_list
operand (the first non-option argument). Each
.B \-e
argument, and the bare
.I pattern_list
operand, is itself split into one or more patterns on any embedded
<newline> bytes it contains; each line of a
.B \-f
.I pattern_file
contributes exactly one pattern, and a missing final newline in that
file is tolerated. Multiple
.B \-e
and
.B \-f
options may be combined; every pattern they contribute is collected in
the order their options appear on the command line.
.PP
Unless
.B \-F
is given, each pattern is a regular expression: a basic regular
expression by default, or an extended regular expression under
.BR \-E .
This library recognizes only the C/POSIX locale, so
.BR \-i 's
case-folding is a plain byte-value case fold, not a locale-aware one,
and no character class or collation stronger than that locale actually
has is available to a pattern.
.PP
No option accepted by
.B grep
is ever bundled with another; each is always its own separate
argument, never combined the way
.B \-i
and
.B \-n
could be combined into
.BR \-in .
.PP
When no
.I file
operand is given, or one is given as
.BR \- ,
standard input is read and reported (for
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-c ,
and the line-prefix below) as "(standard input)". Each selected line
is preceded by its
.I file
operand's name (or "(standard input)") followed by a colon whenever
more than one
.I file
operand was given on the command line \(em this depends on how many
.I file
operands were given, not on how many of them actually contained a
match.
.PP
A line's own trailing newline, if it had one, is preserved on output
exactly as read; a final line with no trailing newline is written
without one being added.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-E
Interpret every pattern as an extended regular expression, instead of
a basic regular expression. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-F .
.TP
.B \-F
Interpret every pattern as a fixed string, matched literally, not as a
regular expression. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-E .
.TP
.B \-c
Instead of the matching lines themselves, write a count of matching
lines to standard output: one count per
.I file
operand (prefixed with that file's name if more than one operand was
given), or a single count if reading standard input. Mutually
exclusive with
.B \-l
and
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-i
Match without regard to the case of alphabetic characters.
.TP
.B \-l
Instead of the matching lines themselves, write only the name of each
.I file
(or "(standard input)") that contains at least one matching line, once each, as soon as its
first match is found. Mutually exclusive with
.B \-c
and
.BR \-q .
.TP
.B \-n
Precede each output line with its 1-based line number within its own
file, followed by a colon.
.TP
.B \-q
Write nothing to standard output; stop reading as soon as any line
matches (without even finishing the file it was found in, or reading
any further files). Mutually exclusive with
.B \-c
and
.BR \-l .
See EXIT STATUS for how
.B \-q
changes the exit status rules.
.TP
.B \-s
Suppress the diagnostic ordinarily written when a
.I file
operand cannot be opened for reading. Does not suppress the
diagnostic
.B grep
writes when a pattern's own matching attempt against one input line
runs out of budget (see APPLICATION USAGE) \(em that condition is not
one of the error messages this option's own description scopes it to.
.TP
.B \-v
Select the lines that do
.B not
match any pattern, instead of the ones that do.
.TP
.B \-w
Select only matches that form whole words: a candidate match is
accepted only when the byte immediately before its first character
(if any) and the byte immediately after its last character (if any)
are both outside the set of alphanumeric characters and the
underscore character.
This option is not required by POSIX, but is implemented because
essentially every widely-deployed
.B grep
provides it and scripts routinely assume it exists.
.TP
.B \-x
Select only matches that span an entire line, from its very first
byte to its very last.
.TP
.BI \-e " pattern_list"
Add the pattern(s) in
.I pattern_list
(see DESCRIPTION) to the collected pattern list. May be given more
than once.
.I pattern_list
may follow
.B \-e
directly in the same argument, or as the next argument.
.TP
.BI \-f " pattern_file"
Add one pattern per line of
.I pattern_file
to the collected pattern list. May be given more than once.
.I pattern_file
is opened as an ordinary pathname; unlike a
.I file
operand,
.B \-
here does not mean standard input.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I pattern_list
The pattern (or patterns; see DESCRIPTION) to match, used only when
neither
.B \-e
nor
.B \-f
was given.
.TP
.I file
A pathname of a file to search, or
.B \-
for standard input. If no
.I file
operand is given, standard input is searched.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.B grep
enforces a bounded amount of work when attempting to match a pattern
against one input line. If that budget is exhausted before a match (or
the absence of one) can be determined, the line is treated as not
matching that particular pattern \(em matching continues with the
remaining patterns and the remaining lines of input, rather than the
whole run being aborted \(em but a diagnostic is written once and the
overall exit status is forced nonzero for the run.
.B \-s
does not suppress that diagnostic.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one line was selected.
.TP
.B 1
No lines were selected, and no error occurred.
.TP
.B 2
An error occurred: invalid usage, an invalid pattern, a
.I file
that could not be opened or read (even when
.B \-s
suppressed the message), an out-of-memory condition, or a pattern that
ran out of matching budget on some line (see APPLICATION USAGE). With
.BR \-q ,
this exit status is used only if no line was ever selected either;
if a line was selected, the exit status is 0 even if an error also
occurred during the same run.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sed (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR expr (1),
.BR find (1)
